INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(95,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12327529','Current status of cardiology fellow training based on COCATS-4 framework: A cross-sectional study from three tertiary care institutes across India.','Gawalkar AA, Paramasivam G, Akkineni KP, Vijayvergiya M, Karki P, Ghati N, Padmakumar R, Narang R, Vijayvergiya R.','2025','preselected preselected preselected pgimer chandigarh aiims kasturba kmc manipal pgimer preselected pgimer kmc aiims cyanotic cyanotic undeniably championing eisenmenger ihj preselected talwar ihj ozair ehac140 ihj beller bonow beller bonow nasca philibert nejmsr1200117 parwani s12968 sivaram julien krim vahanian beyersdorf praz rigsby hlavacek nasci jcct ruel naemsp e771 e862 gara drazner dilsizian arrighi udelson s12350 narula chandrashekhar dilsizian clyman ogrinc headrick lypson frohna woolliscroft gawalkar kasinadhuni lehl vijayvergiya nmji','Abstract Background The Core Cardiology Training Symposium (COCATS) guidelines, endorsed by the American College of Cardiology (ACC) emphasize competency-based training and establish milestones to be met during cardiology training. However, India lacks such guidelines for training cardiology fellows. Objectives The study aimed to evaluate the self-declared competencies of cardiology fellows across multiple preselected domains as per the COCATS-4 recommendations. Methods The study was conducted at three premier institutions across India between June 2022 and December 2024. A total of 52 cardiology fellows who had recently completed their 3-year core cardiology training course were included in the study. These candidates completed a questionnaire that included a checklist assessing different levels of competency in various preselected aspects of cardiovascular medicine. Candidates were also asked about their awareness of COCATS-4 standards and cardiology training in India. Results While all trainees reported Level 1 competency in cardiac catheterization, heart failure, and adult congenital heart disease, a significantly lower percentage of trainees achieved Level 1 competency in nuclear medicine (1.9 %), cardiac MRI (3.8 %), and cardiac CT (17.3 %). At Level 2, cardiac catheterization (73.1 %) and echocardiography (63.5 %) had the highest self-declared competencies, whereas nuclear medicine (3.8 %), cardiac MRI (0 %), and cardiac CT (0 %) had the lowest. Only 23.1 % of trainees were aware of COCATS-4, and 82.7 % believed that standardized guidelines would improve cardiology training in India. Conclusions The results emphasize the wide variability in self-reported competencies of cardiology trainees across India and highlight the deficiencies in certain core areas like cardiovascular imaging. Keywords: Cardiology in India, Cardiology training, COCATS, Competency, Curriculum 1. Introduction The core cardiology training in India began more than half a century ago. Over the years, there has been a gradual increase in the number of both public and private institutions providing training in the field of core cardiology as DM or DNB. After completing training, these medical professionals are considered eligible and competent to provide independent clinical care to patients with cardiovascular diseases. Although these training courses are designed to impart adequate knowledge and skills to the trainees, there is a lack of standardization in India. 1 , 2 , 3 On the contrary, the United States has a rigorous system of regularly updated recommendations in various core areas of cardiovascular medicine. The fourth version of Core Cardiology Training Statement (COCATS) is the present iteration. They were initially published in 1995 as recommendations for training in adult cardiovascular medicine. 4 The recommendations were gradually updated to include peripheral interventions and cardiac MRI in the second iteration, and electrophysiology and cardiac CT were added in the third iteration. 5 , 6 Recently, there has been an emphasis on competency-based training. The objective is to assess competency across core areas in cardiology and establish milestones to be met as the fellows progress in training (COCATS-4). 7 , 8 The fourth iteration focuses especially on research during training, the use of multimodality imaging, and critical care cardiology. COCATS 4 has divided training into three levels (level I to III). The emphasis of COCATS-4 is on Level I competency. Level I competency are those skills and knowledge that all cardiology fellows must attain during a standard 3-year training program. Level II competency is the one which is achieved when a fellow spends 6 months or more during the 3-year training on a specific aspect of cardiology and is objectively evaluated. Level III competency are those skills and knowledge that requires additional training beyond the standard 3 years training course (Objective competency criteria within each domain has been described in Supplementary material 1 ). The first step in developing similar recommendations for cardiology training in India is to assess the existing level of competence based on the established COCATS framework. Understanding the self-perceived competencies of cardiology trainees can offer valuable insights into the strengths and gaps in current training programs. This cross-sectional observational study was conducted to evaluate the self-declared competencies of cardiology trainees across multiple preselected domains, as outlined in the COCATS-4 recommendations. It is the first study to assess the strengths and gaps in the competencies achieved in cardiology training in India. 2. Methods The study was conducted at three premier institutions across India: the Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research (PGIMER), Chandigarh; the All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi; and Kasturba Medical College (KMC), Manipal. The responses were received from eligible candidates between June 2022 and December 2024. The institutional ethics committee of all three institutes approved the study, with PGIMER serving as the coordinating institute (Ref. No. IEC/2021/1726). A total of 52 candidates who had completed their 3-year core cardiology training course (DM Cardiology) were included in the study. After informed and written consent, these candidates filled out a questionnaire that contained a checklist for different levels of competency in various preselected aspects of cardiovascular medicine, i.e. electrocardiography, echocardiography, nuclear medicine, cardiac computed tomography, cardiac MRI, peripheral artery disease, cardiac catheterization, electrophysiology, heart failure, critical care and adult congenital heart disease ( Supplementary material 1 ). The questionnaire was prepared per the COCATS-4 framework with prior permission. Level I competency has been described objectively in the COCATS -4 guidelines for electrocardiography, echocardiography, nuclear medicine, computed tomography, cardiac MRI, peripheral artery disease, cardiac catheterization, electrophysiology, heart failure, critical care cardiology and adult congenital heart disease. Level II competency has been described objectively for echocardiography, nuclear medicine, computed tomography, cardiac MRI, peripheral artery disease, cardiac catheterization, electrophysiology and heart failure. Level III competency has been objectively described only for echocardiography, cardiac catheterization and adult congenital heart disease. These categories, which were objectively described at all three levels, were included in the questionnaire ( Supplementary Material 1 ). The responses were recorded as yes (score of 1) or no (score of zero). The number of yes responses for each question was tabulated for analysis. Candidates were also asked three questions about their awareness of COCATS-4 standards and cardiology training in India ( Supplementary Material 1 ). 3. Results A total of 52 eligible candidates participated in the study, comprising 29 from PGIMER, 14 from KMC, and 9 from AIIMS. Candidates fulfilling all the criteria for a particular level of competency in a category were considered to possess corresponding “self-declared complete competency”. The number of candidates deemed to possess self-declared complete competency is reported in Table 1 and depicted in Fig. 1 , categorized by the level of competency and core area of cardiology training involved. All of the candidates ( n = 52, 100 %) felt they had achieved complete level I competency in cardiac catheterization, heart failure and adult congenital heart disease. The categories with the least self-declared level I competency were nuclear medicine, cardiac MRI and computed tomography, with only 1 (1.9 %), 2 (3.8 %) and 9 (17.3 %) fellows declaring competence, respectively. For level II competency, categories with a maximum number of self-declared complete competencies were cardiac catheterization ( n = 38, 73.1 %) followed by echocardiography ( n = 33, 63.5 %). Like level I, the lowest number of self-declared complete competencies was seen in computed tomography ( n = 0), cardiac MRI (0) and nuclear medicine ( n = 2, 3.8 %). Table 1. Number of candidates who carry self-declared complete competencies in each category and the mean scores. No. of candidates who carry self-declared complete competency in each category (%), Total = 52 Mean (±SD) scores in different categories (Range: 0–1) Level I Competency Electrocardiography-Level I 48 (92.3 %) 0.98 ± 0.06 Echocardiography-Level I 37(71.2 %) 0.89 ± 0.17 Nuclear Cardiology- Level I 1(1.9 %) 0.03 ± 0.15 Cardiac computed tomography-Level I 9(17.3 %) 0.36 ± 0.37 Cardiac MRI-Level I 2(3.8 %) 0.32 ± 0.28 Peripheral artery disease-Level I 34(65.4 %) 0.72 ± 0.41 Cardiac catheterization-Level I 52(100 %) 1 ± 0 Electrophysiology-Level I 28(53.8 %) 0.85 ± 0.17 Heart failure-Level I 52(100 %) 1 ± 0 Critical Care-Level I 30(57.7 %) 0.78 ± 0.27 Adult cyanotic congenital heart disease-Level I 52(100 %) 1 ± 0 Level II Competency Echocardiography-Level II 33(63.5 %) 0.80 ± 0.30 Nuclear Cardiology- Level II 2(3.8 %) 0.17 ± 0.25 Cardiac Computed tomography-Level II 0 0.12 ± 0.69 Cardiac MRI-Level II 0 0.02 ± 0.09 Peripheral artery disease-Level II 16(30.8 %) 0.58 ± 0.33 Cardiac catheterization-Level II 38(73.1 %) 0.83 ± 0.32 Electrophysiology-Level II 4(7.7 %) 0.40 ± 0.31 Heart failure-Level II 25(48.1 %) 0.48 ± 0.50 Level III competency Echocardiography-Level III 23(44.2 %) 0.66 ± 0.33 Cardiac catheterization-Level III 31(59.6 %) 0.59 ± 0.49 Adult cyanotic congenital heart disease-Level III 40(76.9 %) 0.76 ± 0.42 Open in a new tab Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Horizontal bar chart showing the number of candidates who self-declared their competence levels across various domains of cardiology training. Blue bars indicate the number of candidates who declared themselves to have complete competence. Grey bars indicate the number of candidates who self-declared to be not completely competent. The mean scores in each category are shown in Table 1 . The results of the mean competency score align with the ‘number of candidates with complete competencies in each category, as reported earlier. Only 12 candidates (23.1 %) were aware of COCATS-4 guidelines for cardiology training, while 24 candidates (46.2 %) were unaware of any national guidelines for cardiology training. Of the 52 candidates, 43 (82.7 %) believed introducing curriculum guidelines, such as COCATS-4, would improve cardiology training. 4. Discussion The results of this survey highlight significant variability in self-declared competency levels across different domains of cardiology training in India. While all participants (100 %) reported Level I competency in cardiac catheterization, heart failure, and adult congenital heart disease, considerably lower Level 1 competency rates were observed in nuclear medicine (1.9 %), cardiac MRI (3.8 %), and cardiac CT (17.3 %). For Level 2 competency, cardiac catheterization (73.1 %) and echocardiography (63.5 %) were the most commonly achieved skills, whereas only 3.8 % reported Level 2 competency in nuclear medicine and none in cardiac CT and CMR. The trend persisted for Level 3 competencies. These results align with existing literature emphasising the predominance of procedural training in traditional modalities over advanced imaging techniques. 9 , 10 There are subtle variations in the results among the participants from the three institutes. It is important to emphasize that the COCATS guidelines were implemented to ensure Level 1 competence across all core areas of cardiology training to ensure that fellows were sufficiently skilled to manage patients at the end of the training period independently. 11 In this regard, it is particularly concerning that core areas of cardiology training, especially those related to imaging modalities, have very low levels of self-declared Level I competence. The importance of imaging modalities has undeniably increased over the past few decades, with recent guidelines championing imaging as Class I recommendations for several indications, including chronic coronary syndromes and management of valvular and congenital heart disease. 12 , 13 , 14 Similarly, the emphasis on critical care cardiology training is justified in the latest guidelines because adequate training can improve the likelihood of good outcomes in these sicker patients. 15 Ultimately, these guidelines aim to improve patient care; therefore, efforts must be made to ensure their implementation to the greatest extent possible. Several factors may have contributed to these disparate results across different domains. One major factor is the accessibility of training resources. Cardiac catheterization and echocardiography are fundamental procedures in cardiology, with well-established infrastructure, experienced faculty, and adequate patient exposure available in most cardiology training programs. In contrast, advanced imaging modalities like cardiac MRI, nuclear cardiology, and cardiac CT are recent and dynamic additions that have not had adequate penetration into training programs and curricula. 16 , 17 Moreover, CT and MRI-based imaging have traditionally been the primary focus in the training of radiology residents. Cardiology trainees have not been given adequate time, opportunities, and responsibilities during their core 3-year training to improve their imaging skills, despite the availability of CT/MRI in the vast majority of medical institutions that impart cardiology training in India. Furthermore, faculty expertise plays a crucial role in shaping the competencies of trainees. Institutions with dedicated specialists in advanced imaging techniques are more likely to offer structured training and mentorship in these fields. In centres where such expertise is limited, fellows may not receive adequate guidance or opportunities to develop competency in these subspecialties. A rotation through various concerned departments for the fellows will be beneficial, rather than relying solely on the expertise of individual faculty for all matters. Notably, only 23.1 % of respondents were aware of the COCATS-4 training guidelines, and 46.2 % acknowledged the lack of national cardiology training standards in India. However, 82.7 % of participants agreed that the introduction of structured training guidelines similar to COCATS-4 would improve cardiology training. These findings underscore the need for standardized competency frameworks to bridge training gaps and ensure uniform expertise across institutions. Like the COCATS-4 guidelines, national guidelines formulated by the National Medical Commission (NMC) must rely on the six pillars of cardiology training: medical knowledge, patient care, interpersonal and communication skills, professionalism, practice-based learning, and systems-based practice. 18 , 19 Task forces should be formed with experts in various cardiology subspecialties, guiding the development of guidelines and curriculum with a special emphasis on endemic diseases such as rheumatic heart disease and Eisenmenger syndrome. Policy changes by the NMC, which mandate the availability of imaging modalities in all institutions providing cardiology training and ensuring that fellows in training receive adequate exposure, will go a long way in improving competency levels in these categories. Periodic assessments in core areas of cardiology training will foster competence and learning among the fellows. The NMC should administer a standardized, objective exam at the end of training for licensing and certification purposes, ensuring uniformity. All medical institutions providing cardiology training should be required to regularly update the progress and assessment of trainees to the NMC. The NMC website ( https://www.nmc.org.in/information-desk/for-colleges/pg-curricula-2/ ) for training does not have a separate curriculum-based guideline for cardiology, whereas such guidelines have been established for most super-specialities, including neurology, gastroenterology, and rheumatology. Bidirectional feedback, where trainees and faculty mutually evaluate and provide constructive feedback to one another, will enhance and accelerate competency-based training. 20 , 21 , 22 5. Limitations Although this is a novel study, only 52 participants could be included. The three institutes participating in the study are centres of excellence in India, with high patient volumes and access to the latest technologies and advancements. Our results are not generalizable to other Indian institutes because of a marked disparity in the training curriculum across India. This study was based on self-reported competency levels subject to certain inherent biases. Further research involving a larger cohort from multiple centres might provide deeper insights into training needs and inform policy recommendations. 6. Conclusion This study underscores the variability in self-declared competency levels among cardiology trainees across different domains, highlighting significant gaps in advanced imaging training. The findings emphasize the urgent need for standardized competency-based guidelines to ensure comprehensive cardiology education and uniform skill development across institutions in India. Data availability All the data relevant to this study are available with the corresponding authors and can be provided at reasonable request. Financial support None. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Footnotes Appendix A Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ihj.2025.05.010 . Appendix A. Supplementary data The following is the Supplementary data to this article: Questionnaire based on the COCATS-4 framework, the candidates responses for different levels of competency in various preselected aspects of cardiovascular medicine and their awareness of COCATS-4 framework and cardiology training in India. Multimedia component 1 mmc1.docx (32.2KB, docx) References 1. Talwar K.K. Time to revisit super-speciality training programs in cardiology in India. Indian Heart J. 2015;67(6):518–520. doi: 10.1016/j.ihj.2015.10.378. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bhat V., Ozair A., Kumar A., Kalra A. Advanced subspecialty cardiology training in India in 2022: challenges and future directions. Eur Heart J. 2022;43(24):2264–2266. doi: 10.1093/eurheartj/ehac140. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kalra A. Postgraduate cardiovascular disease training in India requires standardization. Indian Heart J. 2016;68(2):235. doi: 10.1016/j.ihj.2015.12.001. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. COCATS Guidelines Guidelines for training in adult cardiovascular medicine, core cardiology training Symposium. June 27-28, 1994. American College of cardiology. J Am Coll Cardiol. 1995;25(1):1–34. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Beller G.A., Bonow R.O., Fuster V., Core Cardiology Training S. ACC revised recommendations for training in adult cardiovascular medicine. Core Cardiology Training II (COCATS 2). (Revision of the 1995 COCATS training statement) J Am Coll Cardiol. 2002;39(7):1242–1246. doi: 10.1016/s0735-1097(02)01795-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Beller G.A., Bonow R.O., Fuster V., et al. ACCF 2008 recommendations for training in adult cardiovascular medicine core cardiology training (COCATS 3) (revision of the 2002 COCATS training statement) J Am Coll Cardiol. 2008;51(3):335–338. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2007.11.008. American College of Cardiology F, American Heart A, American College of Physicians Task Force on Clinical C. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Halperin J.L., Williams E.S., Fuster V. COCATS 4 introduction. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2015;65(17):1724–1733. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2015.03.020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Nasca T.J., Philibert I., Brigham T., Flynn T.C. The next GME accreditation system--rationale and benefits. N Engl J Med. 2012;366(11):1051–1056. doi: 10.1056/NEJMsr1200117. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Parwani P., Chen T., Allen B., et al. Challenges and opportunities for early career medical professionals in cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) imaging: a white paper from the Society for Cardiovascular Magnetic Resonance. J Cardiovasc Magn Reson. 2023;25(1):65. doi: 10.1186/s12968-023-00968-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Weissman G., Lee J., Sivaram C.A., et al. The current state of cardiovascular imaging training: results of the cardiovascular imaging program directors'' survey. JACC Cardiovasc Imaging. 2020;13(7):1638–1639. doi: 10.1016/j.jcmg.2020.02.010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Sinha S.S., Julien H.M., Krim S.R., et al. Cocats 4: securing the future of cardiovascular medicine. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2015;65(17):1907–1914. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2015.03.015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Vahanian A., Beyersdorf F., Praz F., et al. 2021 ESC/EACTS guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease: developed by the task force for the management of valvular heart disease of the European society of cardiology (ESC) and the European association for cardio-thoracic surgery (EACTS) Rev Esp Cardiol. 2022;75(6):524. doi: 10.1016/j.rec.2022.05.006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Han B.K., Rigsby C.K., Hlavacek A., et al. Computed tomography imaging in patients with congenital heart disease Part I: rationale and utility. An expert consensus document of the society of cardiovascular computed tomography (SCCT): endorsed by the society of pediatric radiology (SPR) and the North American Society of Cardiac Imaging (NASCI) J Cardiovasc Comput Tomogr. 2015;9(6):475–492. doi: 10.1016/j.jcct.2015.07.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Rao S.V., O''Donoghue M.L., Ruel M., et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI guideline for the management of patients with acute coronary syndromes: a report of the American College of cardiology/American heart association joint committee on clinical practice guidelines. Circulation (New York, N Y) 2025;151(13):e771–e862. doi: 10.1161/CIR.0000000000001309. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. O''Gara P.T., Adams J.E., 3rd, Drazner M.H., et al. COCATS 4 task force 13: training in critical care cardiology. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2015;65(17):1877–1886. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2015.03.027. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Dilsizian V., Arrighi J.A., Cohen R.S., Miller T.D., Solomon A.J., Udelson J.E. COCATS 4 task force 6: training in nuclear cardiology : endorsed by the American society of nuclear cardiology. J Nucl Cardiol. 2015;22(4):812–825. doi: 10.1007/s12350-015-0162-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Narula J., Chandrashekhar Y.S., Dilsizian V., et al. COCATS 4 task force 4: training in