INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(103,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12312004','Toward a Roadmap for Best Practices in Pediatric Preventive Cardiology: A Science Advisory From the American Heart Association.','Perak AM, Baker-Smith C, Hayman LL, Khoury M, Peterson AL, Ware AL, Zachariah JP, Raghuveer G; American Heart Association Council on Hypertension; Council on Cardiovascular and Stroke Nursing; Council on Lifelong Congenital Heart Disease and Heart Health in the Young.','2023','proliferated pollination proliferated nonlead deanonymized divisional converged fulfills divisional fulfills cimt cimt misapplication hypertriglyceridemia fibrates hypertensives physiologists passionate welcoming physiologists callbacks discourages physiologists expediting ppcs geographies freeing divisional disincentive fiercely realign tirelessly resourcing resourcing underresourced spur nihms2083034 supplemental_tables nihms2083034 appendix_b nihms2083034 appendix_c 105kb nihms2083034 appendix_a ahajournals bhavsar goodell sally canter preetha balakrishnan barcia shoa saar danon ferranti britni delva harahsheh mahgerefteh shawyntee mccloskey mccrindle ranjit ponniah prendergast madhu pushpa shivaram starc sumski elaine urbina vande kappelle kendra reprints wolterskluwer perak hayman zachariah raghuveer e000120 perak carissa nemours hayman esperion bempedoic r21 geetha raghuveer justin zachariah shawyntee wolfson mccrindle amryt esperion chiesi ultragenyx elaine urbina targus ravanbakht skelton e20173459 wiltz loustalot ritchey mm6727a2 perak ferranti andes rolka imperatore e194498 labarthe rosamond foraker grandner berenson wattigney bogalusa nejm199806043382302 sinaiko ikonen juonala kartiosuo lehtimaki magnussen viikari jsa nejmoa2109191 bricker schieken voors gard cresanta berenson bogalusa nejm198601163140302 pasquali thibault gaynor gaies shahian ferranti rodday cull rodday mccrindle birken ferranti foraker grandner lavretsky perak e43 s213 s256 2107c ferranti steinberger ameduri gooding mietus mitsnefes e603 e634 schipper ferranti e2022057956 nihms2083034 supplemental_tables nihms2083034 appendix_b nihms2083034 appendix_c 105kb nihms2083034 appendix_a','Abstract Cardiovascular disease risk factors are highly prevalent among youth in the United States and Canada. Pediatric preventive cardiology programs have independently developed and proliferated to address cardiovascular risk factors in youth, but there is a general lack of clarity on best practices to optimize and sustain desired outcomes. We conducted surveys of pediatric cardiology division directors and pediatric preventive cardiology clinicians across the United States and Canada to describe the current landscape and perspectives on future directions for the field. We summarize the data and conclude with a call to action for various audiences who seek to improve cardiovascular health in youth, reduce the burden of premature cardiovascular disease, and increase healthy longevity. We call on heart centers, hospitals, payers, and policymakers to invest resources in the important work of pediatric preventive cardiology programs. We urge professional societies to advocate for pediatric preventive cardiology and provide opportunities for training and cross-pollination across programs. We encourage researchers to close evidence gaps. Last, we invite pediatric preventive cardiology clinicians to collaborate and innovate to advance the practice of pediatric preventive cardiology. Keywords: AHA Scientific Statements, cardiovascular diseases, delivery of health care, health risk behaviors, heart disease risk factors, pediatrics Risk factors for cardiovascular disease (CVD) are highly prevalent among contemporary adolescents in the United States and Canada. Among youth 12 to 19 years of age in the United States, 39% have over-weight or obesity, 53% have abnormal lipids, 18% have prediabetes, and 15% have elevated blood pressure. 1 – 5 Such risk factors in childhood are robustly associated with premature CVD events. 6 , 7 Thus, there is a substantial need for primary CVD prevention efforts during childhood. The first pediatric preventive cardiology (PPC) programs began in the 1980s, temporally related to both the surge in obesity and landmark studies identifying coronary atherosclerosis in youth at autopsy. 8 – 10 As the burden of childhood CVD risk has continued to increase, these programs have proliferated across the United States and Canada. However, there remains a general lack of clarity on “best practices” or even viable options for structuring PPC programs, embedding them in a complex system of other specialists, ensuring their sustainability, and improving patient outcomes. On behalf of the American Heart Association, the authors of this document initiated a line of work to characterize current practices in PPC, identify high-value practices and gaps, and develop strategies to close the gaps. As an initial step toward these goals, we conducted electronic surveys of pediatric cardiology division directors and PPC clinician leaders across the United States and Canada. The current advisory reports the results of this survey and puts forth a call to action to advance this line of work and ultimately the practice of PPC. METHODS The authors of this document consisted of a group of AHA volunteers who are clinicians from the United States and Canada with diverse experiences in PPC. Survey topics and specific queries, including both structured and unstructured (free text) items, were developed using a combination of literature review and group discussion. REDCap surveys were designed and then piloted by the authors and select other individuals (eg, associate division directors and nonlead PPC clinicians who would not be completing the deployed surveys) and refined on the basis of feedback. Surveys were deployed through an email with a cover letter describing the surveys’ purposes. First, we anonymously surveyed division directors of pediatric cardiology programs associated with university hospitals in the United States and Canada. The ≈3-minute survey queried climate of practice, including division characteristics and personal opinions toward PPC, and the presence or absence of a PPC program ( Appendix A in the Supplemental Material). An optional, identifiable survey allowed the naming of their local PPC clinician(s) ( Appendix B in the Supplemental Material). Division directors were identified through a comprehensive list of pediatric cardiology programs. Three email reminders were sent at ≈1-week intervals. Second, we surveyed PPC clinicians from centers across the United States and Canada (regardless of whether they were associated with a university hospital, ie, a more diverse sample than the division directors’ survey). The ≈20-minute survey queried current practices, such as program scope, team, and therapies, and perspectives for the future ( Appendix C in the Supplemental Material); it was anonymous but included an option for respondents to identify themselves. PPC clinicians were identified through a combination of: (1) deanonymized responses identifying local PPC clinicians in the division director surveys; (2) web-based searches and phone calls querying each of the academic pediatric cardiology centers; (3) queries to the National Lipid Association’s Pediatric Working Group email listserv; and (4) personal contact through professional networks. The lead PPC clinician from each program was invited to complete the survey, and up to 4 email reminders were provided at ≈ 2-week intervals. PPC clinician survey data were tabulated, both overall and stratified on the basis of the size of the division’s cardiac surgical program (small center: <150 surgeries/y; medium center: 150–349 surgeries/y; large center: >349 surgeries/y 11 ; or other: no surgery). The stratification is intended to allow clinicians viewing the data to consider findings in the context of their own center size. Findings are reported descriptively, consistent with the purpose of this advisory; no formal statistical testing was performed. This project was reviewed by the Institutional Review Board of the Ann & Robert H. Lurie Children’s Hospital of Chicago, which gave an exempt determination. RESULTS Part 1. Division Directors’ Survey: Practice Climate in Academic Pediatric Cardiology Divisions Surveys were sent to 109 division directors on August 24, 2021; 53 (49%) responded before the survey closure on September 24, 2021. Results are shown in Table 1 . PPC programs were established at 65% of large centers, 61% of medium centers, and 17% of small centers. In general, division directors valued PPC programs’ contributions for CVD risk factor modification, public health, and community outreach (>80%). Most respondents (72%–74%) also viewed PPC programs as fulfilling divisional and departmental missions and providing training opportunities for fellows. Some valued PPC for patient volume (53%), research funding generation (47%), or revenue (32%). Division directors commonly endorsed that lipid disorders (91%), hypertension (79%), family history of premature CVD (79%), and obesity (76%) should be addressed by a PPC program and that the program team should include a self-identifying preventive cardiologist (89%), registered dietician (91%), advanced practice practitioner (74%), and social worker (72%). At least half of respondents endorsed that an exercise physiologist (62%), program registered nurse (55%), and psychologist (51%) should be part of the team, whereas more than one-fourth endorsed that a behavioral therapist (45%), genetic counselor (38%), vascular testing technologist (38%), program administrative assistant (32%), and research associate (28%) should be on the team. Free text comments converged on themes around the PPC landscape being served in part by other pediatric subspecialists, primary care, and dietitians, and PPC’s perceived low revenue generation and thus difficulty assigning resources to PPC. Table 1. Results of Pediatric Cardiology Division Directors’ Survey Survey results N (%) Overall Cardiac surgical program size Small Medium Large Number responded 53 (100) 18 (34) 18 (34) 17 (32) Yes, division has a dedicated preventive cardiology program 25 (47) 3 (17) 11 (61) 11 (65) Number (proportion) responding agree or strongly agree * that a dedicated PPC program is a core part of a pediatric cardiology division 31 (58) 7 (39) 12 (67) 12 (71) Number (proportion) responding agree or strongly agree * that PPC is valuable for a given domain Public health: prevention of adult cardiovascular disease in the population 43 (81) 13 (72) 18 (100) 12 (71) Community: outreach, advocacy, and engagement by members of the PPC team 44 (83) 13 (72) 18 (100) 13 (76) Patients: better clinical outcomes for cardiovascular disease risk (eg, dyslipidemia, hypertension) 44 (83) 13 (72) 18 (100) 13 (76) Health system: entry point for patients/families into the system for other revenue-generating clinical services 28 (53) 11 (61) 10 (56) 7 (41) Division of pediatric cardiology: fulfills divisional mission 38 (72) 9 (50) 16 (89) 13 (76) Division of pediatric cardiology: better local/national reputation as a heart center 37 (70) 12 (67) 12 (67) 13 (76) Division of pediatric cardiology: provides fellowship training opportunities 38 (72) 11 (61) 14 (78) 13 (76) Department of pediatrics: fulfills departmental mission 39 (74) 14 (78) 13 (72) 12 (71) Institution: research funding 25 (47) 8 (44) 9 (50) 8 (47) Health system: revenue generation for PPC services 17 (32) 7 (39) 6 (33) 4 (24) Conditions that should be addressed by a PPC program Lipid disorders 48 (91) 14 (78) 17 (94) 17 (100) Hypertension 42 (79) 15 (83) 13 (72) 14 (82) Family history of premature cardiovascular disease 42 (79) 10 (56) 17 (94) 15 (88) Obesity and genetic obesity syndromes 40 (76) 11 (61) 15 (83) 14 (82) Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease 14 (26) 3 (17) 7 (39) 4 (24) Bariatric surgery liaison 21 (40) 4 (22) 10 (56) 7 (41) Insulin resistance/prediabetes 22 (42) 6 (33) 9 (50) 7 (41) Metabolic syndrome 32 (60) 8 (44) 13 (72) 11 (65) Personnel that should be part of a PPC program Self-identifying preventive cardiologist (MD or DO) 47 (89) 16 (89) 18 (100) 13 (77) Advanced practice practitioners (NP, PA) 39 (74) 12 (67) 15 (83) 12 (71) Registered dietician 48 (91) 16 (89) 17 (94) 15 (88) Exercise physiologist 33 (62) 8 (44) 13 (72) 12 (71) Life coach/motivational specialist or behavioral therapist 24 (45) 9 (50) 9 (50) 6 (35) Psychologist 27 (51) 6 (33) 10 (56) 11 (67) Social worker 38 (72) 11 (61) 13 (72) 14 (82) Genetic counselor 20 (38) 5 (28) 5 (28) 10 (59) Vascular testing technologist 20 (38) 3 (17) 8 (44) 9 (53) Research associate 15 (28) 3 (17) 8 (44) 4 (24) Program administrative assistant 17 (32) 4 (22) 6 (33) 7 (41) Program registered nurse 29 (55) 8 (44) 10 (56) 11 (65) Open in a new tab Values are stated as n (%). DO indicates doctor of osteopathy; MD, medical doctor; NP, nurse practitioner; PA, physician assistant; and PPC, pediatric preventive cardiology. See text for details and definitions. * Rated on a 5-point Likert scale as agree (4) or strongly agree (5). Part 2. PPC Lead Clinicians’ Survey: Current Practices and Future Aspirations Surveys were sent to 73 PPC clinicians on May 10, 2022; 41 (56%) responded before survey closure on July 21, 2022. Results are shown in Tables 2 to 6 and Supplemental Tables 1 to 5 . Table 2. Selected Results of the PPC Clinician Surveys: Program Scope and Teams Results N (%) Overall 41 (100) Cardiac surgical program size Small 6 (15) Medium 13 (32) Large 17 (41) Other * 5 (12) Program scope (see also Supplemental Table 1 ) Number of clinics per month (1=1 full day or 2 half-days of 1 clinician) No dedicated preventive cardiology clinics 5 (12) 2 (33) 1 (8) 1 (6) 1 (20) 0.5–1/mo 4 (10) 2 (33) 0 1 (6) 1 (20) 1.5–4/mo 16 (39) 1 (17) 6 (46) 6 (35) 3 (60) 4.5–8/mo 7 (17) 1 (17) 4 (31) 2 (12) 0 >8/mo 9 (22) 0 2 (15) 7 (41) 0 Length of the program’s wait list No significant wait list, near-immediate availability 2 (5) 0 1 (8) 0 1 (20) <1 mo 12 (29) 2 (33) 4 (31) 3 (18) 3 (60) 1 to <3 mo 13 (32) 2 (33) 4 (31) 6 (35) 1 (20) 3 to <6 mo 8 (20) 1 (17) 2 (15) 5 (29) 0 ≥6 mo 6 (15) 1 (17) 2 (15) 3 (18) 0 Program growth phase Currently actively trying to grow the program 15 (37) 4 (67) 6 (46) 2 (12) 3 (60) In a steady state, not trying to grow and not overwhelmed 9 (22) 1 (17) 2 (15) 4 (24) 2 (40) Overwhelmed with referrals 14 (34) 1 (17) 4 (31) 9 (53) 0 Program Teams (see also Supplemental Table 2 ) Personnel considered part of the program (even part-time) Self-identifying preventive cardiologist(s) (MD or DO) 37 (90) 6 (100) 13 (100) 15 (88) 3 (60) Advanced practice practitioner(s) (NP, PA) 19 (46) 2 (33) 5 (38) 10 (59) 2 (40) Registered dietician(s) 33 (80) 5 (83) 10 (77) 15 (88) 3 (60) Program RN(s) 14 (34) 0 5 (38) 9 (53) 0 Exercise physiologist(s) 13 (32) 1 (17) 5 (38) 6 (35) 1 (20) Genetic counselor(s) 13 (32) 1 (17) 6 (46) 6 (35) 0 Program administrative assistant(s) 10 (24) 1 (17) 3 (23) 5 (29) 1 (20) Research associate(s) 6 (15) 0 3 (23) 2 (12) 1 (20) Vascular testing technologist(s) (eg, CIMT or PWV) 5 (12) 0 3 (23) 2 (12) 0 Psychologist(s) or behavioral therapist(s) 4 (10) 1 (17) 2 (15) 1 (6) 0 Social worker(s) 3 (7) 0 3 (23) 0 0 Personal trainer(s) 1 (2) 0 1 (8) 0 0 Life coach(es) or motivational specialist(s) 1 (2) 0 1 (8) 0 0 Total number of MD/DO-equivalent FTEs devoted to the program, median (25th–75th percentile) 0.2 (0.2–0.8) 0.2 (0.1–0.2) 0.2 (0.2–0.6) 0.5 (0.2–1.2) 0.1 (0.05–0.2) Total number of APN/PA-equivalent FTEs devoted to the program, median (25th–75th percentile) 0 (0–0.3) 0 (0–0.2) 0 (0–0.2) 0.05 (0–1) 0 (0–0.1) Fellowship training in PPC offered (formal or informal) 5 (12) 0 0 5 (29) 0 Yes, formal advanced fellowship program in preventive cardiology 2 (5) 0 0 2 (12) 0 Yes, informal advanced fellowship training 3 (7) 0 0 3 (18) 0 No 36 (88) 6 (100) 13 (100) 12 (71) 5 (100) Open in a new tab Results are shown as N (%) except when indicated in the row label. APN indicates advanced practice nurse; CIMT, carotid intima media thickness; DO, doctor of osteopathy; FTE, full time equivalent; MD, medical doctor; NP, nurse practitioner; PA, physician assistant; PPC, pediatric preventive cardiology; PWV, pulse wave velocity; and RN, registered nurse. See text for details and definitions. * Five responding PPC programs were not practicing within a cardiac surgical program. Table 6. Themes in PPC Clinician Surveys: Gaps in Evidence Major themes Subthemes Selected illustrative quotes Need for long-term outcome studies in children with FH Clinical impact of long-term treatment “Misapplication of short-term studies to risk/benefit analysis of long-term treatments.” “Lack of dietary evidence-based literature.” “…just how long an FH patient not being on a statin increases his/her risk of having an early event. Is it dangerous to wait from age 13 to 18 to start a statin a patient with FH?” “Prospective data on the efficacy of treatment and timing for intervention.” Treatment thresholds and targets “Cut points to treat lipids and hypertension that actually lead to reduced cardiovascular events…” Safety of statin therapy “No mid-term outcome safety studies.” The treatment and outcomes of children with acquired and other forms of dyslipidemia Metabolic syndrome “Therapies for high triglyceride/low HDL-C, metabolic syndrome patients.” Lifestyle interventions “Long-term outcomes on the impact of diet and lifestyle for children with heterozygous FH or mixed dyslipidemia.” “Mid-term/long-term outcomes of dietary intervention (eg, plant-based) on dyslipidemia in children.” Hypertriglyceridemia “Use of fibrates and other medications to lower triglycerides” Medication indications and outcomes “Indications for lipid lowering therapy in combined dyslipidemia of obesity.” “Longitudinal follow-up and outcomes of pediatric patients without FH on statin therapy.” Treatment of pediatric hypertension Antihypertensive therapy “Need for long-term outcome data re: antihypertensive agent use” “Need for head-to-head trials of medications” “Indications for starting anti-hypertensives” Target organ damage “Utility of tracking cardiac end-organ damage.” Facilitating lifestyle interventions “Evidence supported interventions that can facilitate lifestyle change, particularly in patients with low resources.” Need for multicenter collaborations “Multicenter studies with intervention outcomes” Open in a new tab FH indicates familial hypercholesterolemia; HDL-C, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol; and PPC, pediatric preventive cardiology. Program Scope Findings on program scope are shown in Table 2 and Supplemental Table 1 . All responding PPC programs received referrals for dyslipidemia management, and PPC was the primary service receiving dyslipidemia referrals among 78% of programs ( Supplemental Table 1 ). PPC programs were also the primary referral program for family history of premature atherosclerotic CVD (78%). In contrast, although 78% of PPC programs received referrals for hypertension, only 12% were considered the primary referral program for hypertension. PPC programs were commonly a secondary referral program for other conditions, such as obesity and metabolic syndrome. The proportion of referrals that were for genetic conditions (such as familial hypercholesterolemia) varied widely. Among respondents, 56% of all PPC programs and 71% of PPC programs at large centers have existed for >10 years. Programs commonly (39% overall) provided 1.5 to 4 dedicated, full days of PPC clinics per month. At least one program of each size reported offering no dedicated PPC clinic, whereas 41% at large centers and 15% at medium centers offered >8 full days of PPC clinics per month. Nearly half (47%) of programs overall, including 17% at small centers, 54% at medium centers, and 66% at large centers, reported at least 40 patient visits per month ( Supplemental Table 1 ). Only 5% of programs had immediately available appointments, whereas wait list times were 3 to 6 months among 20% and >6 months among 15% of programs. More than half at large centers (53%) reported being overwhelmed with referrals, and two-thirds at small centers (67%) reported actively trying to grow their PPC clinics. Program Teams Findings on program teams are shown in Table 2 and Supplemental Table 2 . The PPC teams included at least one self-identifying pediatric preventive cardiologist at 37 of 41 (90%) programs, although the median total effort devoted to PPC by all such cardiologist(s) combined was only 0.2 full-time equivalents (FTE); (75th percentile: 0.8 total FTE; Table 2 and Supplemental Table 2 ). Registered dieticians were common (≈80%) across programs. About half of program teams included advanced practice practitioner(s) (46%, 75th percentile: 0.3 total FTE), and one-quarter to one-third included registered nurse(s) (34%), exercise physiologist(s) (32%), genetic counselor(s) (32%), and administrative assistant(s) (24%). Fewer teams (10%–15% each) included research associate(s), vascular testing technologist(s), or psychologist(s)/behavioral therapist(s). Only 5 programs, all at large centers, offered advanced fellowship training in PPC, either formal (2 programs) or informal (3 programs). Program preventive cardiologists had themselves obtained formal training in PPC among 41% of programs ( Supplemental Table 2 ). Program Services Findings on program services are shown in Supplemental Table 3 . Most programs used same-day dietician consultation, genetic testing in suspected familial hypercholesterolemia, and telehealth services at least some of the time (76%, 76%, and 91% of programs, respectively), and some programs used these services commonly (48%, 51%, and 36% of programs, respectively; Supplemental Table 3 ). Vascular testing was used at least sometimes at 10 programs (31%) and commonly at 3 of those programs (8%), but it was unavailable (40%) or never used (35%) at others. Bariatric surgery, exercise laboratory services, and remote health measurements were also either unavailable or never used at most PPC programs. Program Interventions and Treatments Findings on program interventions and treatments are shown in Supplemental Table 4 . PPC programs used comprehensive therapeutic approaches including pharmacological, physical activity, behavior modification, and social support interventions ( Supplemental Table 4 ). All reported using statins in their PPC programs. Most programs prescribed omega-3 fatty acids (73%) and antihypertensive medications (75%), whereas fewer prescribed step-up lipid-lowering therapies, such as proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitors (30%) and low-density lipoprotein apheresis (20%), or referred patients to enroll in clinical trials (33%). Structured physical activity programs, such as electronically delivered home exercise or exercise handouts, were used in one-third of programs. Some programs reported referring patients to community-based healthy lifestyle programs (35%) or social support programs (28%) or providing between-visit education (25%). Program Review Findings on program review are shown in Supplemental Table 5 . Care processes within PPC programs were defined as standardized practice (written algorithm or shared standard) versus individual decision-making ( Supplemental Table 5 ). Most programs (83%) standardized initial evaluation and testing, and around two-thirds each standardized clinical intervention (ie, treatment thresholds, choice of intervention, and referral to community programs: 58%), criteria for referral to other pediatric subspecialists or specialized testing (58%), follow-up frequency and testing (65%), and transition to adult clinicians (63%). Formal research is ongoing in 48% of programs, with 25% engaged in quality improvement efforts. Perspectives on Program Wins and Challenges PPC clinicians were invited to respond to the open-ended questions “What are the best things about your program and why?” ( Table 3 ) and “What are the biggest challenges for your program?” ( Table 4 ). Dedication and expertise of program team members, including committed nurses, dieticians, genetic counselors, and exercise physiologists, was one major theme on program strengths that recurred across multiple program respondents. Multiple programs reported as strengths that they provide service to their communities or that they participate in research, and some were strengthened by having access to sufficient resources to provide patient care. Table 3. Major Themes Identified Through Free Responses in PPC Clinician Surveys: What Are the Best Things About Your Program? Major themes Selected illustrative quotes Dedication and expertise of team members “We have a passionate and dedicated team and really strive to work with the patients to individualize their care and optimize success.” “Nurturing and welcoming…Offer both in person and telehealth…We provide comprehensive (whole family and whole patient) care. “Our size, diversity of patients and patient diagnoses, many years of experience treating lipid disorders, accessibility to ancillary services such as bariatric surgery, weight programs, etc.” Access to resources “…Committed nurse, dietician, and PA-C. Offer dietician visits with each visit. Nurse coordinator screens referrals and schedules according to our entrance criteria.” “Dedicated support staff for receiving new referrals, scheduling, collecting lab results.” “Option for dietary input and exercise rehab.” “Strong features include ready access to genetic testing and a dietician who is part of the program. Adding a nurse has been invaluable to coordinate testing and follow-up.” Research opportunities “We…offer a very busy clinic and robust research program.” “Clinical trials; team expertise.” Service to community “The size and geographical reach allows us to serve our diverse community. We are strongly integrated with our primary care referring providers.” “Our program has an extensive presence in regional clinics where we are the sole specialty provider for pediatric cardiometabolic health and obesity. This presence has allowed us to reach children who previously could not access these services, grow our program, and has increased awareness of lipid screening …” Open in a new tab PA-C indicates certified physician assistant; and PPC, pediatric preventive cardiology. Table 4. Themes Identified Through Free Responses in PPC Clinician Surveys: What are the Biggest Challenges for Your Program? Major themes Subthemes Selected illustrative quotes Lack of clinical resources Lack of physicians and advanced practice practitioners “We do not have enough providers to see the patients leading to long wait times.” “Overwhelmed by new referrals…” Lack of dieticians, exercise physiologists, and other services “Access to support services such as dieticians, lifestyle clinics, and exercise therapists.” “Access to ancillary services…including sufficient nutritional support and any access to health psychology.” “Lack of social work, psychology, and scant dietician availability.” Lack of clinical support including RN support “We do not have enough resources, time slots, or support to track patients or offer additional support...” “Number of labs to review and patients that require callbacks. The volume is overwhelming and almost impossible to keep up. If an established patient does not get new labs prior to the visit, it is time consuming to call them after the visit…it is like doing the clinic visit twice.” Lack of interest or dedication from health care team “…Lack of enough providers interested in the clinic.” “MD/DO not committed to the program.” Challenges of financial sustainability “Program is not self-supporting. Difficulty generating RVUs and without outside financial support is difficulty to continue. This also leads to inability to provide appropriate social, nutritional, and ancillary support.” “…Financial difficulty…is noted by the trainees and discourages them from following this career pathway.” “Our program is resource-heavy and doesn’t generate significant revenue, especially compared to other components of the congenital cardiology program.” “Lack of insurance coverage for visits.” Lack of patient adherence and resources “Patient no-shows and compliance with lifestyle recommendations.” “Resource poor population [with low] adherence to medications and follow up plan.” Open in a new tab DO indicates doctor of osteopathy; MD, medical doctor; PPC, pediatric preventive cardiology; and RVU, relative value unit. Conversely, lack of resources emerged as a major challenge experienced by other programs, and this encompassed lack of PPC physicians, advanced practice practitioners, nurses, dieticians, exercise physiologists, psychologists, social work, and clinical support staff. Multiple respondents connected the lack of resources with challenges of financial sustainability, citing relatively high resource needs compared with perceived revenue generation. Multiple respondents also described their patients’/populations’ lack of resources and patient/family difficulty with adhering to recommendations as challenges. Perspectives on Gaps in Practice and Evidence Findings are shown in Tables 5 and 6 . Table 5. Priority Ranking to Improve the Practice of Pediatric Preventive Cardiology Priority N (%) 1=highest priority 2 3=medium priority 4 5=low priority 99=Not a priority at all Updated guidelines for comprehensive primary prevention of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease 20 (50) 12 (30) 5 (13) 2 (5) 0 1 (3) Multicenter electronic medical record database/registry study for outcomes of primary prevention interventions 13 (33) 14 (35) 5 (13) 5 (13) 1 (3) 1 (3) Formal research studies of novel care models in primary prevention 13 (33) 13 (33) 9 (23) 4 (10) 0 0 Advanced fellowship training programs for pediatric preventive cardiology 8 (20) 6 (15) 18 (45) 6 (15) 0 2 (5) Other priorities, written in Public awareness and advocacy, data for health care institutions on the cost/benefit of a formal program to support staff/resource development, allocation of resources for prevention, best strategies for improving screening and treatment uptake by primary care clinicians, sharing best practices, a meeting to discuss advances in the field and difficult cases specific to pediatric preventive cardiology clinicians, online education and certification, expediting access for new medications, studies of methods for compliance and retention, uniform protocols for when/how to perform vascular testing Open in a new tab A total of 40 respondents provided rankings. Among predefined options, the specific gap of highest perceived priority to advanced PPC practice was the need for updated guidelines on the comprehensive primary prevention of CVD, with 32 of 40 respondents (80%) ranking this as highest or high priority ( Table 5 ). Many respondents also perceived a highest or high priority for multicenter outcome registries (68%) and studies of novel care models (66%). The need for formal fellowship training was considered high or highest priority by fewer respondents (35%) and was more commonly considered medium priority (45%). Numerous additional gaps were identified through written-in responses, such as public awareness and advocacy for PPC, collaboration of PPC clinicians to share best practices and discuss difficult cases, and other topics. In free-text responses about gaps in evidence, numerous themes emerged ( Table 6 ). The most prevalent themes centered around the need for long-term outcome studies of the treatment of children with inherited or acquired dyslipidemia, including treatment initiation thresholds, treatment targets, the impact on future CVD risk, and the safety of long-term statin therapy starting at various ages in childhood and adolescence. Another theme pertained to the treatment of hypertension, including the need for head-to-head trials of antihypertensive medications and the role of target organ damage assessment. Other major themes identified the need for studies aiming to facilitate implementation of lifestyle interventions, particularly in resource-poor environments, and multicenter studies evaluating the real-world effectiveness of such interventions. DISCUSSION A Call to Action Primary prevention is critical for attenuating acquired CVD, the most common cause of death in the United States, Canada, and worldwide. In response to the increasing burden of CVD risk in children, pediatric cardiologists across the United States and Canada have developed local PPC programs independently over the past few decades. Our findings illustrate that these programs were developed and continue to operate in a climate of high need, low resources, and limited guidance. Coordinated, systematic change across multiple sectors will be required to evolve these programs to fully meet the needs of our youth. Below and in the Figure , we offer several calls to action arising from the survey data. Figure. Calls to action to advance the practice of pediatric preventive cardiology. Open in a new tab PPC indicates pediatric preventive cardiology. High Need for PPC Services The process of atherosclerotic disease begins in childhood, and early modification of CVD risk factors can minimize disease burden throughout the life course. PPCs are well suited to address primary CVD prevention in children on the basis of their training in cardiovascular pathophysiology and the pathways by which varied early life exposures can affect cardiac and vascular function. Of course, in our data both pediatric cardiology division directors and PPC clinicians noted that the PPC landscape is served in part by clinicians in other specialties, such as general pediatricians and pediatric endocrinologists, and PPC clinicians prioritized study of novel care models (which might maximize collaboration, minimize redundancy, and improve outcomes). Nevertheless, multiple lines of evidence confirm the need for focused, dedicated PPC. For example, surveys of general pediatricians in the United States and Canada demonstrated that, although they generally agreed that statins are appropriate to use in youth, few felt comfortable initiating statins themselves, and a lack of local specialists was cited as a barrier to lipid treatment. 12 , 13 In addition, we found that existing PPC programs are often overwhelmed with referrals and have long wait lists. This suggests a large unmet need even where PPC programs exist and potentially even larger unmet need where PPC programs are absent. Calls to Action ( Figure ): Professional societies and foundations can advocate for PPC programs and patients on the basis of the high need. Policymakers, payers, hospitals, and heart centers can invest resources into PPC programs and patients across varied geographies and center sizes. Researchers and funders can study novel care models to maximize collaboration and minimize redundancy across clinical programs and local resources, thereby improving patient outcomes and freeing up resources to serve more patients. Low Resources for PPC Programs In our survey, pediatric cardiology division directors widely recognized the value of PPC programs to public health and divisional/departmental missions. They listed robust human resources as essential for these programs. However, this contrasted with the actual available resources as reported by PPC clinicians. Lack of resources was repeatedly identified by PPC clinicians as a challenge. Division directors highlighted the gap between needed and available PPC resources and cited, in part, the lack of institutional investment in PPC. Some respondents expressed a perception of PPC as low revenue generating, and, although this view is not necessarily backed by data (at least at the level of clinic visit revenue) and was not shared by all respondents, it is important to highlight because even the perception of less revenue generation may serve as a disincentive for centers to create or further develop PPC programs despite high need. It is striking that, whereas division directors commonly identified social workers (72%) and psychologists (51%) or behavioral therapists (48%) as key PPC team members, few existing PPC teams included such experts (≤10% programs for each). CVD risk factors disproportionately burden socially disadvantaged populations and communities who themselves have low resources, and addressing CVD risk factors requires substantial psychosocial and behavioral support, 14 which can be costly. But premature CVD and loss of healthy life-years are also costly to society and are rising in the United States. 15 Lack of resources devoted to PPC represents a structural, institutionalized intensification of CVD risk factor disparities. As a field, we must advocate fiercely for the care we provide, pushing heart centers, hospitals, payers, policymakers, and society to reconsider and realign priorities and resources. Simultaneously, we must innovate tirelessly to create interventions and models of care that are maximally effective and cost-effective in diverse patient populations. Even at the level of PPC physicians and advanced practice practitioners, there are substantial gaps in human resources. Although self-identifying preventive cardiologists were identified by division directors as essential and reported as actually part of PPC teams at similar proportions (≈90%), the total cardiologist FTE devoted to the PPC program was low (median 0.2 and 75th percentile 0.8). This gap was not filled with advanced practice practitioners, because fewer programs (46%) included advanced practice practitioners and devoted FTE was low (0.3 at the 75th percentile). Likewise, 22% of PPC programs offered ≤1 PPC clinics per month. Viewed in the context of the high proportions of the general population of youth with CVD risk factors, it is not surprising that long wait lists are common and that PPC clinicians identified lack of sufficient personnel as a major challenge for their programs. In our survey, only 2 programs reported offering formal PPC fellowship training, with another 3 programs offering informal training. The need for PPC fellowship training programs was ranked as high or highest priority by 35% and medium priority by 45% of PPC clinicians. Likewise, most practicing PPC clinicians were not formally trained themselves. Some expressed desire for online training and certification opportunities in free-text responses. There is also a need for more formal PPC training for other PPC team roles, particularly nurses and advanced practice practitioners. More robust care teams with various clinicians serving different roles may offer opportunities to decrease cost, increase capacity, and improve overall program outcomes. Calls to Action ( Figure ): PPC clinicians can advocate for their patients and programs to their local heart centers and hospitals, and to payers and policymakers through collaboration with professional societies and foundations. PPC clinicians can also engage in research to advance care models and the interventions that we provide. Professional societies and foundations can advocate for PPC programs and patients and can provide education and training opportunities for various PPC clinicians. Researchers and funders can study novel care models and create new interventions to maximize efficacy and cost-effectiveness of PPC clinical care. Policymakers, payers, hospitals, and heart centers can invest resources into PPC programs and patients, working to align resource allocation with need, especially for the most highly affected and socially disadvantaged populations. Limited Guidance for PPC Practice We noted substantial variability in practice patterns across PPC programs. This variation in part reflects a paucity of guidance, and sometimes of underlying evidence, supporting best practices. The most recent comprehensive pediatric primary CVD prevention guidelines were published more than a decade ago in 2011, 16 and respondents indicated a high priority for updating these guidelines. Respondents also identified research priorities to fill evidence gaps and advanced PPC practice, such as specific questions around pharmacotherapies, optimizing lifestyle interventions, novel care models, and real-world, multicenter outcomes of PPC interventions. The observed practice variation and clinician-identified gaps point to potential benefits of PPC programs coming together for collaborative learning and practice advancement. For example, many programs had internally established standardized pathways for the core aspects of care. Sharing and discussing these between programs may help to illuminate best practices, especially if supported with insights from programs’ quality improvement studies. Some interventions or services were used by a minority of programs and learning from the experience of such programs could inform others considering where to best invest limited resources. Furthermore, some programs reported having robust teams and services, and their insights on resourcing and sustainability could be shared across programs to improve the viability of the field overall. Calls to Action ( Figure ): Researchers and funders can prioritize research that fills key evidence gaps identified by respondents. PPC clinicians can collaborate and learn together to advance PPC practice. PPC clinicians can also contribute to research to address evidence gaps. Professional societies and foundations can support updated guideline development and can provide opportunities for PPC clinicians to collaborate and network to advance PPC practice. Limitations The survey data reported here are subject to several limitations. First, as described in the Methods, we used several avenues attempting to identify and contact both cardiology division directors and PPC clinicians across the United States and Canada. However, it is possible that we did not successfully identify all directors and programs, and among those identified and contacted, about half did not complete the surveys. Second, the survey instruments used (for both the division directors and PPC clinicians) are novel and have not been validated. Third, all surveys are vulnerable to numerous biases, including recall and availability bias, and responses may not reflect true practice patterns. Still, we did note consistency of responses between division directors and PPC clinicians at the level of center size, which may indicate some face validity of the survey. Fourth, we presented survey data descriptively and did not perform formal statistical testing, so comparisons are limited. Fifth, there are additional relevant topics in PPC (eg, special populations within PPC 18 , 19 ) that merit future attention but that we chose not to include in this survey to keep the length manageable. Notwithstanding these limitations, to our knowledge, the current document represents the first published overview of the PPC landscape in ≈35 years. 8 Future Directions This project identified current PPC program components, perceived best practices, and critical gaps in evidence and resources from the perspective of pediatric cardiology division directors and PPC clinicians. It can serve as the first groundwork-laying and hypothesis-generating step for future work to identify and attain optimal standards for PPC program development, implementation, and resourcing. Important next steps will include (1) comprehensive review of evidence for best practices; (2) development of updated, evidence-based clinical practice guidelines; (3) generation of expert/clinician consensus for PPC program standards that increase program value, while still encouraging program adaptation to local context; and (4) formulation and implementation of a strategic action plan to achieve these standards. Input and support from multiple sectors will be required. CONCLUSIONS With this advisory, we sought to elevate the voices of PPC clinicians and pediatric cardiology division directors, and their message was clear: PPC services are in high demand but are underresourced and operating with limited guidance. We urge policymakers, payers, hospitals, heart centers, professional societies, foundations, and funders to match investments into PPC to the gravity and urgency of our youths’ high burden of CVD risk. We ask funders and researchers to prioritize filling the gaps in evidence identified by PPC clinicians as critical barriers to progress. We invite PPC clinicians to join forces and pool our collective knowledge and energy to the benefit of the field. We hope that this document will help spur action across multiple sectors to advance primary CVD prevention for the next generation. Supplementary Material Supplemental Tables NIHMS2083034-supplement-Supplemental_Tables.docx (40.6KB, docx) Appendix B NIHMS2083034-supplement-Appendix_B.pdf (48.6KB, pdf) Appendix C NIHMS2083034-supplement-Appendix_C.pdf (105KB, pdf) Appendix A NIHMS2083034-supplement-Appendix_A.pdf (116.3KB, pdf) Supplemental Material is available at https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/suppl/10.1161/HCQ.0000000000000120 Acknowledgments The authors gratefully acknowledge the contributions of Jessica Bhavsar, Lauren Goodell, and Sally Canter in implementing the REDCap surveys. We also gratefully acknowledge all the pediatric cardiology division directors and PPC clinicians who responded to the surveys, including those who did and did not identify themselves. Those individuals who agreed to be identified and acknowledged include the following: Preetha Balakrishnan, John Barcia, Joseph Block, Julie Brothers, Shoa L. Clarke, Rubin Cooper, Saar Danon, Sarah de Ferranti, Britni Delva, Ashraf S. Harahsheh, Alan B. Lewis, Christina M. Fink, Joseph Mahgerefteh, Shawyntee Mayo, John P. McCloskey, Brian McCrindle, Ranjit Philip, Kumar Ponniah, 